\"placed_after_references\": true,\n    \"pre_bibliography_clearpage_present\": true,\n    \"rendered_checked\": false,\n    \"rendered_heading_present\": false,\n    \"rendered_visible_character_count\": 0,\n    \"substantive_element_count\": 11,\n    \"visible_character_count\": 6714\n# Paper validation feedback\n\n- generated_at: 2026-11-11T10:26:33.936409+00:00\n- paper_dir: `work/results/r02/claude/20261111_085834/paper`\n- ok: `False`\n\n## Hard errors\n- figure validation failed: figures[1] figure_type does not match strategy for final_comparison; figures[2] figure_type does not match strategy for variant_profile\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `6714`\n- substantive_element_count: `11`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `work/results/r02/claude/20261111_085834/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/r02/claude/20261111_085834/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `75`\n- resolved_references: `33`\n- anchored_references: `42`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `8`\n\n## Soft warnings\n- figures[1] exceeds series budget in panel 1: 3 > 2\n- figures[1].min_font_size_pt below minimum: 7.5 < 8.0\n- figures[1] PNG width too small: 1675 < 2400\n- figures[1] PNG height too small: 660 < 700\n- figures[2].min_font_size_pt below minimum: 6.0 < 8.0\n- figures[2] PNG width too small: 1622 < 2400\n- figures[3].min_font_size_pt below minimum: 6.0 < 8.0\n- figures[3] PNG width too small: 1595 < 2400\nReliability profile and residual failures}\nThe headline margin should be read together with the residual gaps. On exchangeable cases the mean marginal under-coverage gap is 0.0017, compatible with the finite-sample guarantee; because the gap is one-sided and averaged over a few seeded runs, this is not a test of the guarantee and does not show coverage is tight. The mean set-size ratio is 0.209. The shift-case marginal gap is 0.0536 and the worst-slice gap is 0.0675, so both residual failures are larger than the exchangeable gap by more than an order of magnitude. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.shift_coverage_gap]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.worst_slice_coverage_gap]]\n\nBecause Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss} is linear in the case-mean metrics, the mean loss of 0.1584 decomposes as follows: the set-size term contributes $0.25\\times0.209=0.0522$, the slice term $0.25\\times0.0675=0.0169$, and the remainder, $0.0893$, is the marginal term, equivalent to a mean clipped marginal gap of $0.179$ in units of $\\alpha$ over all cases. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThis subtraction relies on the evaluator aggregating the components exactly as reported, and $\\alpha$ is not retained, so we do not convert it to a coverage number. Since exchangeable-case gaps are small, the marginal term is plausibly dominated by shifted cases, but we cannot establish this without per-case results. By this subtraction the implied largest residual cost is marginal under-coverage rather than set size, subject to the caveats above. We do not attribute the residual shift or slice gaps to statistical noise, to the density-ratio estimation, or to a validity failure of the weighting: no gate-level diagnostics are retained.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.78\\textwidth]{figures/loss_decomposition.pdf}\n\\caption{Decomposition of the sealed final mean loss of the studied procedure into the three terms of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss}. The set-size and worst-slice terms are computed from the retained metrics; the marginal term is the remainder. The best baseline's total loss is shown without decomposition because its components are not retained.}\n\\label{fig:decomp}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:variants} shows the three development configurations. Adding the gated weighting to Mondrian LAC (V1 to V2) raised the development score from 0.8683 to 0.9192, reduced the shift-case gap from 0.0613 to 0.0201 and the worst-slice gap from 0.0497 to 0.0376, and left the exchangeable marginal gap unchanged at 0.00028; set size grew from 0.166 to 0.181. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\nThe per-seed paired differences in score are 0.064, 0.043 and 0.045, all positive, but with three seeds this is descriptive rather than inferential. Because the components are changed together we can say only that the V2 configuration outperformed V1 on these development cases, not which element of the weighting step is responsible.\n\nV3 reproduced V2 in every retained field: score, all six metrics and all three per-seed scores. The V3 source falls back to the V2 path verbatim when its calibration-only gate does not activate, which would give this outcome; but whether the gate was inactive or the quantile-regression thresholds were exercised and rejected is not recorded, so the cause of the tie remains unknown. We therefore report no evidence for or against quantile-regression thresholds.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/variant_profile.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-time comparison of three nested configurations (V1: Mondrian LAC; V2: plus gated weighting, the studied procedure; V3: plus quantile-regression thresholds). Panels show shift-case marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio (lower is better) and per-seed scores over seeds 101--103 (higher is better; bar is the mean). V3 coincides with V2 in every retained value. Development cases and seeds differ from the sealed final data.}\n\\label{fig:variants}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\nThe development score of the selected procedure (0.9192) is higher than its sealed final score (0.8416), and the final shift gap (0.0536) and set-size ratio (0.209) exceed the development values (0.0201, 0.181). The two evaluations use different seeds and case sets, and sealed cases may be harder; the retained artifacts do not isolate the cause, so we make no statement about selection effects or over-fitting. Only the sealed numbers should be used for conclusions; Table~\\ref{tab:devfinal} in the Supplementary Information lists both sets side by side.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained, and a different weighting could change the ranking. The interval half-width is about 0.011 and the margin is roughly twice the per-seed standard deviation of the procedure's scores (0.019), but baseline per-seed scores are not retained, so a seed-level paired effect size cannot be computed.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n(i) \\emph{Real data.} No real-data run exists; behaviour on classifier outputs from natural datasets is untested.\n(ii) \\emph{Baseline identity.} Only the best baseline score is retained; we cannot say which baseline it is or how the others compare.\n(iii) \\emph{Cases and families.} Per-case and per-family results, $\\alpha$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases are unknown; the bootstrap interval does not cover variation across case designs.\n(iv) \\emph{Mechanism.} The comparison among V1--V3 is a design comparison on three seeds and does not isolate the contribution of any single component of the weighting step; V3 provides no information on quantile regression.\n(v) \\emph{Validity versus statistics.} The residual shift and slice gaps are reported as measured; the diagnostics needed to separate estimation error in the density ratio, small-sample noise and violated assumptions are not available, and candidate-side diagnostics are unverified.\n(vi) \\emph{Range.} The tested range is limited by the evaluator ($K\\le100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le2000$); nothing is claimed about larger $K$, very small calibration sets, or simplex-valued regression targets.\n(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n\n\\paragraph{Evidence inventory.} Table~\\ref{tab:inventory} (Supplementary Information) separates available from unknown quantities, so gaps are not mistaken for null results.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.}\nThe synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to choose the configuration, so development numbers may be optimistic; this was not measured, and the cause of the development-to-sealed drop is not isolated. The sealed evaluation used data disjoint from development by content hash; we have no retained record that it was run only once, and we could not verify which of the two tied configurations (V2 or V3) the sealed metrics belong to beyond their ranking position, so the frozen configuration's identity is not independently verified. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n\n\\paragraph{What would change the conclusion.} The positive margin would be weakened if per-baseline results showed that the best baseline wins on the slice or size axis and loses only on a few shifted cases, or if the sealed cases were mostly shifted. It would be strengthened by per-case margins whose intervals all exclude zero, by gate logs showing the gate closed on exchangeable cases, and by replication on cases generated from different families.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On sealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard baselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95\\% interval of 0.0274--0.0499, with no failed episodes and all negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not remove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposition implies, by subtraction under the evaluator's aggregation, that the marginal term is the largest component (the marginal term is not measured directly, and $\\alpha$ and per-case results are unavailable). The conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline comparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-baseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping, gate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done here.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Procedure specification}\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} lists the constants of the studied procedure as they appear in its source, and Algorithm-style pseudo-code follows.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/V2/program.py#constants]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Constants of the studied procedure (from the V2 source; see the identity caveat in Section~\\ref{sec:method}).}\n\\label{tab:hyper}\n\\begin{tabular}{llr}\n\\toprule\nComponent & Constant & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nScore & nonconformity & $1-p_k(x)$ \\\\\nMondrian & class-conditional if & $K\\le10$ \\\\\nMondrian & confidence bins ($K>10$): max bins / min rows per bin & 10 / 80 \\\\\nMargin & pooled / per-group multiplier $z$ & 0.7 / 0.6 \\\\\nShrinkage & pseudo-count $n_0$ toward pooled threshold & 20 \\\\\nGate & min. test rows before domain fit & 40 \\\\\nGate & weights used only if $\\beta>$ & $10^{-3}$ \\\\\nDomain classifier & ridge penalty / cross-fit folds / features $d$ & 1 / 2 / 5 \\\\\nWeights & clip range (multiplicative) & $[1/8,\\,8]$ \\\\\nFeatures & standardised clip & $\\pm8$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Pseudo-code.}\n\\emph{Calibrate:} compute scores $s_i=1-p_{y_i}(x_i)$; define groups (label if $K\\le10$, else confidence bin); compute per-group margin thresholds $q_g$ and the pooled threshold, with shrinkage; standardise summary features with calibration moments and assign calibration rows to two folds at random.\n\\emph{Predict (per test chunk):} append the chunk's features; if at least 40 test rows are available, cross-fit the domain classifier on the two folds, compute $\\mathrm{LR}$ and $\\beta$; if $\\beta\\le10^{-3}$ or any quantity is non-finite, return $\\{k:1-p_k\\le q_{g}\\}$ with unweighted thresholds; else compute weights $w_i$ (calibration) and $w_j$ (test), form for each test row the weighted group thresholds with $w_j$ at $+\\infty$, and return $\\{k:1-p_k\\le q_{g}(x_j)\\}$. The wire protocol limits calibration to two chunks of at most 1000 rows and test data to four chunks.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\nThe search-time evaluator generates data with the logit model of Section~\\ref{sec:setup}; the sealed final plane uses the same scoring, baseline and bootstrap code with different cases and seeds. Slices require at least 30 test points. Controls: C1 permutes calibration labels; C2 permutes test labels when scoring; C3 replaces all probabilities by the uniform vector. In the evaluator, a control episode whose permutation changes fewer than half of the labels is counted as a failed control (\\texttt{vacuous\\_control}), which invalidates the run. The coverage tolerance for exchangeable cases is $3\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/(n_{\\mathrm{cal}}+2)+1/n_{\\mathrm{test}})/n_{\\mathrm{seeds}}}$. The final evaluator source file is not separately retained, so statements about the final plane rely on the plane-conditional branches of the shared evaluator and on the qualification outcome.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py#aggregate]]\n\n\\subsection{Full development and per-seed tables}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-time metrics for the three nested configurations (seeds 101--103; lower is better for gaps and size ratio, higher for score). Controls passed fraction was 1.0 and failed episodes 0 for all three.}\n\\label{tab:dev}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nConfig. & Score & Exch. gap & Worst-slice gap & Shift gap & Size ratio \\\\\n\\midrule\nV1 Mondrian LAC & 0.8683 & 0.00028 & 0.0497 & 0.0613 & 0.166 \\\\\nV2 + gated weighting & 0.9192 & 0.00028 & 0.0376 & 0.0201 & 0.181 \\\\\nV3 + QR thresholds & 0.9192 & 0.00028 & 0.0376 & 0.0201 & 0.181 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Per-seed scores. Final seeds are sealed-evaluation runs (duration in seconds; what the durations cover is not recorded); development seeds are for V1/V2 (V3 identical to V2).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Score & Seed (dev.) & V1 / V2 score \\\\\n\\midrule\n9001 & 0.8121 (7.6 s) & 101 & 0.8519 / 0.9163 \\\\\n9002 & 0.8495 (4.3 s) & 102 & 0.8547 / 0.8980 \\\\\n9003 & 0.8478 (4.3 s) & 103 & 0.8984 / 0.9433 \\\\\n9004 & 0.8620 (4.3 s) & & \\\\\n9005 & 0.8365 (4.3 s) & & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The selected procedure on development data (seeds 101--103) and on sealed final data (seeds 9001--9005). Different cases and seeds; shown side by side for transparency, not as a paired comparison.}\n\\label{tab:devfinal}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Development & Sealed final \\\\\n\\midrule\nComposite score & 0.9192 & 0.8416 \\\\\nExchangeable marginal gap & 0.0003 & 0.0017 \\\\\nWorst-slice gap & 0.0376 & 0.0675 \\\\\nShift-case marginal gap & 0.0201 & 0.0536 \\\\\nSet-size ratio & 0.181 & 0.209 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Full-set score: $1-(0.5\\cdot0+0.25\\cdot0+0.25\\cdot1)=0.75$ for every case (analytic).\n\\item Relative loss reduction: $(0.1971-0.1584)/0.1971=19.6\\%$. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\n\\item Expected one-sided shortfall factor: $f(0)=0.399$, $f(0.6)=0.169$, $f(0.7)=0.143$. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#margin_constants]]\n\\item Development paired differences V2$-$V1: 0.064, 0.043, 0.045 (mean 0.051); V3$-$V2: 0, 0, 0.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility and what is not retained}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Evidence inventory for the sealed final evaluation.}\n\\label{tab:inventory}\n\\begin{tabular}{p{0.46\\textwidth}p{0.42\\textwidth}}\n\\toprule\nAvailable & Not retained (unknown) \\\\\n\\midrule\nAggregate score, best-baseline aggregate score, paired margin and bootstrap interval & Identity and individual scores of the four baselines \\\\\nFive per-seed scores; failed-episode count; control pass fraction & Per-case and per-family results; which cases drive shift and slice gaps \\\\\nExchangeable, shift, worst-slice and size-ratio aggregates & $\\alpha$, $K$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases \\\\\nSource of the studied procedure and of the evaluator code & Gate-activation frequency; cause of the development-to-final drop; cause of the V3--V2 tie \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe figures and derived numbers are produced by \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} and \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_stats.py} from the evaluator's recorded outputs; the candidate sources and the evaluator source are included in the artifact bundle, and the evaluator requires NumPy 2.2.6 (SciPy is optional in the candidate). Development seeds are 101--103 and final seeds 9001--9005; the final runs took 4--8 seconds each. The sealed final case manifest and the per-baseline results are not retained, so the final numbers cannot be regenerated from the retained files and must be read as evaluator-reported values. No external dataset was used (so no data licence applies), and no real-data experiment was run, including the CIFAR-10 option offered by the task. Bibliographic descriptions in Section~\\ref{sec:related} rely on titles and venues in the retrieval metadata only. Citations to the split/Mondrian, score-function, covariate-shift and compositional-data sources (Section~\\ref{sec:related}) support background statements only; the retrieval metadata record titles, venues and years, and the implementation details of the baselines and of the studied procedure come from the evaluator and candidate sources, not from those papers.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used in this work in two roles. First, an LLM-driven research agent proposed, implemented and evaluated three candidate configurations (V1--V3 here) against the evaluator and wrote the initial analysis. Second, an LLM drafted this manuscript from the evaluator outputs and the retained sources. All reported numbers are copied or arithmetically derived from evaluator outputs.\n\n\\end{document}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb15487a-b4e5-46f4-a97a-1b8322af54df","uuid":"3736f4ca-c7f0-4d69-bdfc-41955031540e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:26:43.645Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"bibtex_key_count\": 30,\n  \"citation_count\": 30,\n  \"claim_count\": 25,\n  \"error\": \"figure validation failed: figures[1] figure_type does not match strategy for final_comparison; figures[2] figure_type does not match strategy for variant_profile\",\n  \"evidence_validation\": {\n    \"bundle_path\": \"work/results/r02/claude/20261111_085834/paper/scientific-evidence\",\n    \"bundle_present\": true,\n    \"checked\": true,\n    \"dangling\": [],\n    \"hard_errors\": [],\n    \"paper_token_count\": 59,\n    \"summary\": {\n      \"anchored_references\": 42,\n      \"dangling_references\": 0,\n      \"manifest